# Messages of lib/checks/40-snap.sh. Format and rules: see common.lang.

section.40-snap=Snap packages

# ---- SNAP-ALL ----
SNAP-ALL.partial.1=snap not installed or snapd not running

# ---- SNAP-ERRORS ----
SNAP-ERRORS.warn.title={n} failed snap operation(s) recently
SNAP-ERRORS.warn.impact=A snap whose update fails stays on its old version, without the fixes of the newer one (security fixes included). Snap tries again on its own, but the same cause usually makes it fail again.
SNAP-ERRORS.warn.act.1=Lists the recent snap operations (installations, updates, removals) with their result. The ID is in the first column. Read-only.
SNAP-ERRORS.warn.act.2=Shows the steps of one operation, given its ID, and which step failed. Read-only.
SNAP-ERRORS.warn.act.3=Updates all snaps now, once the cause is fixed (for example after closing the application that blocked its update). A snap that misbehaves after its update can be put back to its previous version with 'sudo snap revert' followed by its name.
SNAP-ERRORS.warn.caution=Look at the date of each error in the evidence below: an old error may already have been fixed by a later update that worked. Run the first command again to see whether the problem is still there.
SNAP-ERRORS.ev.1=snap changes (errors only)
SNAP-ERRORS.ok.title=No failed snap operation in recent history
SNAP-ERRORS.partial.1=snap operation history not readable
SNAP-ERRORS.net.impact=The errors name the Snap Store or a network failure: most likely the store, or the connection of this computer to it, was unavailable when snap tried to update. The snaps stay on their current version until an update works, which usually happens on its own at the next attempt.
SNAP-ERRORS.net.act.1=Lists the recent snap operations with their result and date, to see whether a later update worked. The ID is in the first column. Read-only.
SNAP-ERRORS.net.act.2=Shows the steps of one operation, given its ID, and the network error it met. Read-only.
SNAP-ERRORS.net.act.3=Once the connection works (a website opens normally), updates all snaps now instead of waiting for the next attempt. A snap that misbehaves after its update can be put back to its previous version with 'sudo snap revert' followed by its name.
SNAP-ERRORS.net.caution=There is nothing to repair on this computer if the errors stopped once the network came back. If they continue while the network works, check for a proxy or a firewall that blocks api.snapcraft.io.
SNAP-ERRORS.ev.2=Network errors found in the operations

# ---- SNAP-REFRESH ----
SNAP-REFRESH.held.title=Snap updates are on hold
SNAP-REFRESH.held.impact=Snap normally updates its snaps on its own, several times a day. While updates are on hold, they keep their current version, without the fixes published since (security fixes included).
SNAP-REFRESH.heldsome.title=Updates on hold for {n} snap(s)
SNAP-REFRESH.heldsome.impact=The snaps listed below stay at their current version, without the fixes published since (security fixes included), until their hold is lifted. The other snaps are updated as usual.
SNAP-REFRESH.held.act.1=Shows when snaps were last updated, when the next update is planned, and until when updates are on hold. Read-only.
SNAP-REFRESH.held.act.2=Lifts the hold on all snaps: automatic updates start again. Can be undone with 'sudo snap refresh --hold'.
SNAP-REFRESH.held.act.3=Lifts the hold on one snap, listed below. Can be undone with 'sudo snap refresh --hold' followed by its name.
SNAP-REFRESH.held.caution=The hold may have been set on purpose: before a presentation, on a metered connection, or to keep a version that works. Lift it only if you know why it was set, or if that reason is gone.
SNAP-REFRESH.ev.1=Hold on all snaps
SNAP-REFRESH.ev.all=all snaps, until {until}
SNAP-REFRESH.ev.2=Snaps held one by one
SNAP-REFRESH.stale.title=Snaps not updated for {days} days
SNAP-REFRESH.stale.impact=Snap normally updates its snaps on its own, several times a day. The last update was on {date}: the snaps miss the fixes published since then (security fixes included).
SNAP-REFRESH.stale.act.1=Shows when snaps were last updated and when the next update is planned. Read-only.
SNAP-REFRESH.stale.act.2=Lists the recent snap operations with their result, to find an update that failed. Read-only.
SNAP-REFRESH.stale.act.3=Updates all snaps now. A snap that misbehaves after its update can be put back to its previous version with 'sudo snap revert' followed by its name.
SNAP-REFRESH.stale.caution=A computer that stays switched off or offline for a long time does not update its snaps: in that case, the first update after it comes back is normal and nothing else is needed.
SNAP-REFRESH.ev.3=snap refresh --time
SNAP-REFRESH.ok.title=Snaps updated {days} day(s) ago
SNAP-REFRESH.partial.1=snap update schedule not readable (snap refresh --time failed)
SNAP-REFRESH.partial.2=date of the last snap update unknown

# ---- SNAP-BRANCH ----
SNAP-BRANCH.warn.title={n} snap(s) follow a branch made for another Ubuntu release (this one is {version})
SNAP-BRANCH.warn.impact=Some snaps follow a channel made for one Ubuntu release (its name ends with ubuntu- and the release number). These ones follow the branch of a release other than {version}, usually the release before an upgrade: they no longer receive the updates made for this one.
SNAP-BRANCH.warn.act.1=Shows the channels this snap is published in, and the one it follows. Read-only (it asks the Snap Store).
SNAP-BRANCH.warn.act.2=Moves the snap to the main channel of its track (the target is listed below for each snap): snap then installs the version published for everyone. Can be undone: 'sudo snap revert' followed by its name goes back to the previous version, and the same command with the old channel shown below follows it again.
SNAP-BRANCH.warn.caution=If the first command shows a branch for this release (ubuntu- followed by your release number), you can follow it instead, by putting its full name after --channel=. Handle one snap at a time.
SNAP-BRANCH.ev.1=Snaps, channel followed, proposed channel
SNAP-BRANCH.ok.title=No snap follows a branch of another Ubuntu release than {version}
SNAP-BRANCH.partial.1=snap channel branches not checked (Ubuntu version number unknown)
SNAP-BRANCH.partial.2=some snap channels are shortened by snap list and could not be read in full

# ---- SNAP-DUPLICATE ----
SNAP-DUPLICATE.conflict.title=Software installed both as a snap and as a deb package
SNAP-DUPLICATE.conflict.impact=Two copies of the same service run side by side and compete for the same resource. For example, two Bluetooth services claiming the same name on the system's internal message bus (D-Bus): the device can then work erratically, and updates of the snap keep failing.
SNAP-DUPLICATE.conflict.act.1=Shows the services provided by the snap and whether they are running. Read-only.
SNAP-DUPLICATE.conflict.act.2=Shows what the snap is connected to (other snaps, system resources), to check that nothing depends on it before removing it. Read-only.
SNAP-DUPLICATE.conflict.act.3=Removes the snap and keeps the deb package. On a desktop, the deb is usually the one to keep for system services. Snap saves a copy of the snap's data first, listed by 'snap saved', and the snap can be installed again with 'sudo snap install' followed by its name.
SNAP-DUPLICATE.conflict.caution=Remove only one of the two copies, and only after the second command showed nothing that depends on it. If several programs are listed below, handle them one at a time.
SNAP-DUPLICATE.ev.1=Duplicates with a service on both sides
SNAP-DUPLICATE.coexist.title=Software installed both as a snap and as a deb package (no service conflict)
SNAP-DUPLICATE.coexist.impact=Both copies live side by side without competing services, which is often intended (snapd, for example). Keeping both uses disk space, and it can be unclear which of the two starts when you launch the program.
SNAP-DUPLICATE.coexist.act.1=If you use only one of the two copies, you can remove the other. If you do not know why both are there, leave them: nothing is broken.
SNAP-DUPLICATE.coexist.act.2=Before removing the snap, shows what it is connected to (other snaps, system resources), to check that nothing depends on it. Read-only.
SNAP-DUPLICATE.ev.2=Duplicates

# ---- SNAP-PUBLISHER ----
SNAP-PUBLISHER.info.title=Snaps from non-verified publishers
SNAP-PUBLISHER.info.impact=The Snap Store has not checked the identity of these publishers. This is not necessarily a problem (many snaps are maintained by their community), but a snap runs the code its publisher put in it, so you should trust each one.
SNAP-PUBLISHER.info.act.1=Shows the publisher of the snap, its description and its contact or web page. Read-only.
SNAP-PUBLISHER.info.act.2=Remove the snaps you do not use or whose publisher you do not know, with 'sudo snap remove' followed by the name. Keep the others.
SNAP-PUBLISHER.ev.1=Snaps

# ---- SNAP-CONFINEMENT ----
SNAP-CONFINEMENT.info.title=Snaps running without strict confinement
SNAP-CONFINEMENT.info.impact=Most snaps run in a closed space (confinement) that limits what they can reach on the computer. 'classic' snaps run without it, with the same access as a regular application: this is normal for development tools and command-line tools. 'devmode' turns that protection off, and is meant only for people developing the snap.
SNAP-CONFINEMENT.info.act.1=Shows the publisher and the description of the snap, to find out why it runs this way. Read-only.
SNAP-CONFINEMENT.info.act.2=A snap in 'devmode' that you did not install yourself for development deserves a closer look: if you do not need it, remove it with 'sudo snap remove' followed by its name.
SNAP-CONFINEMENT.ev.1=Snaps
